Bound the GIC register-write-pending wait, so it fails loudly instead of hanging.

Project: qnx-linux-dual-vm-proxy, Phase 3b, 2026-09-10.
Applies to: src/hardware/startup/lib/public/aarch64/gic.h in the QNX SDP 8.0
hypervisor-guest-arm BSP (Apache-2.0 source; modification is what
NC QDL v7 4.1(iii) grants).

WHY

`wait_for_rwp()` is an unbounded busy-wait, and the library's own comment says
so: `//XXXX: check for never clearing`. It is entered four times on the boot CPU
during `gic_v3_initialize()` and `gic_v3_gicc_init()`.

On every previous validation in this project that was harmless, because the GIC
had just come out of reset under QEMU. This board is the first case where the
same code meets a GICv3 that Linux configured, used, and left enabled with
device interrupts possibly still pending. If the pending bit never clears there,
the loop never exits.

That would be survivable on most boards. On this one it is the worst available
outcome, and both halves of that are measured rather than assumed: the watchdog
does not fire after this kexec hand-over, so a spin needs a human to pull the
power; and a cold power cycle wipes the DRAM region holding everything printed
up to that point. A hang therefore costs the board session *and* the evidence
explaining it. See results/orin-native-port/20260909T1100Z/m0-hang-watchdog.md.

WHAT THIS CHANGES

A deadline, and nothing else. The wait still spins on the same bit and still
exits the instant it clears, so a working GIC behaves exactly as before. When
the bit does not clear it calls `crash()`, which prints through the same console
path as everything else — into the black box as well as the mailbox — and then
reaches this board's `crash_done()`, which issues a PSCI reset rather than
parking in `wfi`. So the failure becomes: a named message saying which register
never settled, a warm reset, and the whole log waiting in pstore on the next
boot.

The bound is an iteration count rather than a real deadline because this runs
before the timer is initialised and there is no calibrated clock to spend yet.
Ten million iterations is far longer than any real propagation and far shorter
than a human noticing the board is gone.

diff --git a/src/hardware/startup/lib/public/aarch64/gic.h b/src/hardware/startup/lib/public/aarch64/gic.h
--- a/src/hardware/startup/lib/public/aarch64/gic.h
+++ b/src/hardware/startup/lib/public/aarch64/gic.h
@@
 static __inline__ void
 wait_for_rwp(paddr_t const addr, unsigned const rwp_bit) {
-	//XXXX: check for never clearing
-	do {
-		// Wait for the RWP bit to clear
-	} while(in32(addr) & rwp_bit);
+	/*
+	 * Bounded (qnx-linux-dual-vm-proxy, 2026-09-10). The original loop is
+	 * unbounded and the comment it carried -- "check for never clearing" --
+	 * is exactly the case this board can produce: a GIC that Linux left
+	 * enabled, re-initialised from scratch. A spin here cannot be recovered
+	 * on this hardware and takes the log with it, so it is a crash instead.
+	 */
+	unsigned long __rwp_limit = 10000000UL;
+
+	while (in32(addr) & rwp_bit) {
+		if (--__rwp_limit == 0) {
+			crash("gic: RWP never cleared at %P (bit %x)\n", addr, rwp_bit);
+		}
+	}
 }
